==English==
{{was wotd|2016|March|14}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|nudius}} {{m|la|tertius}}, from the phrase ''[[nunc]] [[dies#Latin|dies]] [[tertius]] [[est]]'' (“now is the third day”). Coined by {{w|Nathaniel Ward}} (1578–1652) in ''The Simple Cobler of Aggawam in America'' (1647).<ref>{{cite-book|author=David Mellinkoff|title=The Language of the Law|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher={{w|Little, Brown and Company}}|year=1963|page=207|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YBRLAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA207&dq=%22nudiustertian%22&hl=en&sa=X&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22nudiustertian%22&f=false|isbn=978-0-316-56627-8|passage=Ward coined ''nugiperous'' from Latin ''nugae'' (nonsense or foolish), and also ''nudiustertian'' from Latin ''nudius tertius'' (day before yesterday). Both promptly became obsolete.}}</ref>

